  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Ben Rice broke the game open in the ninth, but this team never trailed. Just waited.",
    "what_this_means": "They were chasing runs through eight innings, tied at three. The lineup had chances in the first, when they loaded the bases with nobody out and left two. Again in the seventh, with a runner in scoring position, nothing came. That's two critical windows left closed. But late-game baseball belongs to the moment it's played in, not the mistakes stacked before it. Rice came through when it mattered. A two-run homer that broke a deadlock and turned a tight game into a statement. This is what separates close losses from close wins: one swing at the right moment. Goldschmidt was there all night (3-for-5), and the bullpen didn't blink once they took the lead. The pitching held from the second inning forward, and that stability mattered when the bats finally woke up. One good inning after eight empty ones. That's not a flaw if you win. That's the formula they just proved works.",
